# Shatter — The simulation

This file defines the core the whole game advances on: the fixed timestep, the
order of work inside one tick, and the draws the game makes at random.

## The fixed timestep

The simulation runs on a fixed timestep of `TICK_HZ` (`120`) ticks per second, so
one tick is `TICK_DT` (`1 / 120` of a second) of game time exactly. The simulation
advances in whole ticks and never in a partial one.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The runtime measures how much real time each frame covers and hands it to the
game. The game's own timestep is the one above, so the elapsed time a frame brings
is converted into whole ticks with the remainder carried into the next frame.
{{else}}
The engine measures how much real time each frame covers and hands the game that
delta in seconds; it imposes no timestep of its own. The game's own timestep is
the one above, so the delta a frame brings is converted into whole ticks with the
remainder carried into the next frame.
{{/if}}

## One tick

Every tick runs the following in this order, over every body on the field.

1. Control forces: the ship's facing turns and its thrust acceleration is taken
   along that facing, and the saucer steers.
2. The gravity acceleration from the star, for every pulled body, as
   `specs/gravity.md` states.
3. Velocity: each body's velocity gains the accelerations of steps 1 and 2 over
   `TICK_DT`, and the ship's drag and speed cap are then applied to its velocity
   as `specs/ship.md` fixes.
4. Position: each body's position advances by its velocity over `TICK_DT`.
5. The wrap, bringing each position back into the field.
6. Collision resolution, as `specs/collision.md` states.

Every timer the game keeps counts down by `TICK_DT` on the tick it runs in.

## A render-free core

The simulation advances from the elapsed time the game is handed, independent of a
canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock time. The dependency
runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the renderer. Whether a shot lands,
where a fragment goes, and when a wave turns over are all decided from the field
alone.

## Random draws

The game's rules draw at random in the places below. Each draw's distribution is
stated by the rule it belongs to, and how a build draws it is the build's own.

- A wave's rock positions, their drift directions, and their base drift speeds
  (`specs/progression.md`, `specs/rocks.md`).
- The edge a recycled rock re-enters at and the point on it, and the fresh base
  drift speed it takes (`specs/rocks.md`).
- The edge and the row a saucer enters at, the interval until the next arrival,
  the direction of its first weave, and the aim error of each shot it takes
  (`specs/saucer.md`).

The debug surface `specs/instrumentation.md` specifies poses the outcome of a draw
before the game makes it, and the rule that draws then runs from the posed outcome
exactly as it runs from a drawn one.
